WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.933
Welcome to Module 3 of Defender
X D R Engineering and Operations:

2
00:00:04.933 --> 00:00:06.775
exposure and incidents.

3
00:00:06.775 --> 00:00:10.006
Two queues decide whether the
platform you built is used:

4
00:00:10.006 --> 00:00:14.750
the vulnerabilities waiting for an owner,
and the incidents waiting for an analyst.

5
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.855
In this module you learn
what Defender adds to the

6
00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:21.263
vulnerability program in G C
C, and what it can't do there.

7
00:00:21.263 --> 00:00:25.921
Then you learn how the security
operations center triages, contains,

8
00:00:25.921 --> 00:00:29.762
hands off and closes every
incident, to a table it can show.

9
00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:35.037
The module covers Steps 5 and 6 of the
Defender X D R implementation guide.

10
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:39.880
It takes about thirty-five minutes,
plus Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

11
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:44.967
Why these two together?

12
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:49.492
Because both are queues, and a queue
without an owner is where risk waits.

13
00:00:49.492 --> 00:00:54.684
The vulnerability queue: the remediation
program, its clock and the Known Exploited

14
00:00:54.684 --> 00:00:59.654
Vulnerabilities catalog belong to the
Security Operations and Telemetry book.

15
00:00:59.654 --> 00:01:02.325
The guide adds what Defender contributes,

16
00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:07.359
and its rule that a vulnerability without
an owner is a finding against C M eight,

17
00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:11.555
the inventory control, not just R
A five, vulnerability monitoring.

18
00:01:11.555 --> 00:01:14.380
That's the program's
risk-based recommendation.

19
00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.955
And in G C C, the attack-path
features aren't there.

20
00:01:17.955 --> 00:01:19.721
The incident queue: one queue,

21
00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:23.916
with Sentinel onboarded to the
Defender portal if the agency uses it,

22
00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:27.375
a triage window and a containment
rung for each severity,

23
00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:31.643
and every incident closed with a
classification and a determination.

24
00:01:31.643 --> 00:01:37.712
The response ladder and device isolation
stay in Security Operations and Telemetry;

25
00:01:37.712 --> 00:01:42.080
this module fixes severity,
triage, evidence and hand-off.

26
00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.743
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

27
00:01:48.743 --> 00:01:52.199
Say which exposure features
are available in G C C,

28
00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:55.404
and record the one that
isn't as not applicable.

29
00:01:55.404 --> 00:01:59.039
Prioritize vulnerabilities
with the guide's interim path,

30
00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:02.005
and give every device
group a remediation owner.

31
00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:04.648
Triage an incident to the severity table,

32
00:02:04.648 --> 00:02:07.292
pick its containment
rung, and hand it off.

33
00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:12.255
And export the open incidents, and one
incident with its alerts, as evidence.

34
00:02:12.255 --> 00:02:14.580
You need Module 1's Graph read path.

35
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.702
Skim the Security Operations
and Telemetry book's Chapter 06,

36
00:02:18.702 --> 00:02:24.117
for the remediation program and its clock,
and Chapter 11, for the response ladder.

37
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:27.840
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

38
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.630
Unit 1 is the fact that
shapes this gate in G C C.

39
00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.670
Microsoft Learn's page
on Security Exposure

40
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.203
Management says it's available
in Public Cloud only.

41
00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:44.019
That's the product that
holds attack paths,

42
00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:49.252
the exposure graph and critical asset
management, and it isn't available in G C C.

43
00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:53.844
What G C C does have, from Learn's
Defender for Endpoint government page:

44
00:02:53.844 --> 00:02:58.831
Defender Vulnerability Management, core
and premium, without two features,

45
00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:01.681
report inaccuracy and
request C V E support;

46
00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.164
and Secure Score, with some
recommendations missing.

47
00:03:05.164 --> 00:03:08.475
So a Zero Trust Assessment
test that needs Exposure

48
00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:12.477
Management is recorded as not
applicable in the tenant's cloud.

49
00:03:12.477 --> 00:03:17.200
It goes in the closure register as
a disposition, not a failed control.

50
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.600
The guide's interim path for
attack paths uses what is there.

51
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.376
From the device info table:
exposure level and whether the

52
00:03:28.376 --> 00:03:32.625
device is internet-facing, with
its asset value and machine group.

53
00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:36.143
From Defender Vulnerability
Management: its recommendations,

54
00:03:36.143 --> 00:03:40.525
and the high and critical vulnerabilities
by software and recommended update.

55
00:03:40.525 --> 00:03:44.442
And as the critical-asset list,
the Tier zero inventory from the

56
00:03:44.442 --> 00:03:48.812
Access and Privilege book, standing
in for critical asset management.

57
00:03:48.812 --> 00:03:49.963
Then ownership.

58
00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:54.997
Each device group or device tag maps to a
remediation owner in the decision record,

59
00:03:54.997 --> 00:03:57.884
and the vulnerability
manager works Security

60
00:03:57.884 --> 00:04:00.624
Operations and Telemetry's
queue every week.

61
00:04:00.624 --> 00:04:04.021
One trap from Microsoft
Learn's table reference:

62
00:04:04.021 --> 00:04:06.737
the device T V M tables stay in Defender.

63
00:04:06.737 --> 00:04:10.826
They aren't ingested into Sentinel,
so a Sentinel query that names them

64
00:04:10.826 --> 00:04:14.774
returns nothing, and a silent rule
there is not a clean bill of health.

65
00:04:14.774 --> 00:04:19.520
Run these queries in Defender, or
through Graph, as the lab does.

66
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.395
Unit 2 is the incident queue.

67
00:04:25.395 --> 00:04:29.935
Microsoft Learn's page on listing
incidents says Defender X D R correlates

68
00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:34.182
alerts that share attack techniques
or an attacker into one incident.

69
00:04:34.182 --> 00:04:37.883
With Sentinel onboarded to the
Defender portal, there's one queue.

70
00:04:37.883 --> 00:04:42.457
The severity table is the program's
design, a risk-based recommendation.

71
00:04:42.457 --> 00:04:45.233
A high incident is
triaged within one hour.

72
00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:47.854
Its containment rung is L three, gated:

73
00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:52.507
the playbook prepares the action,
and a named approver releases it.

74
00:04:52.507 --> 00:04:56.297
It's handed off to the incident
response lead and the I S S O,

75
00:04:56.297 --> 00:05:01.295
with the U S cert reporting decision
made under the incident response plan.

76
00:05:01.295 --> 00:05:06.015
A medium incident is triaged within
twenty-four hours, at L two,

77
00:05:06.015 --> 00:05:10.457
advise: enrich it and open a
ticket, and the analyst decides.

78
00:05:10.457 --> 00:05:14.472
A low or informational incident
within seventy-two hours,

79
00:05:14.472 --> 00:05:18.570
at L one, observe, with a tuning
review if it keeps coming back.

80
00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.207
The rungs come from the response ladder in

81
00:05:21.207 --> 00:05:24.580
Security Operations and
Telemetry's Chapter 11.

82
00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.042
Three rules complete the gate.

83
00:05:30.042 --> 00:05:33.320
First, what's never contained
by a playbook: servers,

84
00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.755
privileged accounts and
emergency access accounts.

85
00:05:36.755 --> 00:05:38.943
For those, a human decides.

86
00:05:38.943 --> 00:05:41.355
Second, how an incident closes.

87
00:05:41.355 --> 00:05:45.276
Every incident closes with a
classification and a determination,

88
00:05:45.276 --> 00:05:49.125
and Microsoft Learn says the Graph
incident record carries both,

89
00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:51.905
along with who it's assigned
to and the comments.

90
00:05:51.905 --> 00:05:53.568
Third, the way back.

91
00:05:53.568 --> 00:05:57.244
Containment actions are reversible
from the Action center,

92
00:05:57.244 --> 00:06:01.066
such as releasing a device from
isolation or re-enabling a user,

93
00:06:01.066 --> 00:06:04.080
as Security Operations
and Telemetry describes.

94
00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.642
An incident closed by mistake
is reopened with a comment.

95
00:06:07.642 --> 00:06:10.929
The pilot is two weeks of
the day shift triaging every

96
00:06:10.929 --> 00:06:14.067
incident to the table and
recording time to triage.

97
00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:16.968
The S O C lead reviews the queue daily.

98
00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:21.623
And the evidence: the severity table and
the incident response plan's version,

99
00:06:21.623 --> 00:06:26.278
the monthly incident export with alerts,
classification and timestamps, the

100
00:06:26.278 --> 00:06:28.096
quarterly exercise incident,

101
00:06:28.096 --> 00:06:32.160
and every incident closed outside
its window, with the reason.

102
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.885
Lab 2 runs the guide's validate queries
in a test tenant, never production.

103
00:06:40.885 --> 00:06:43.885
The authors haven't run
them, as the page says.

104
00:06:43.885 --> 00:06:47.010
Every step reads; nothing
in the tenant changes.

105
00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:51.368
You need Module 1's test tenant and
reader account, with vulnerability data

106
00:06:51.368 --> 00:06:55.585
from at least one onboarded device,
and at least one incident in the queue.

107
00:06:55.585 --> 00:06:58.297
Module 1's detection test raises one.

108
00:06:58.297 --> 00:07:00.723
Step one connects in the tenant's cloud.

109
00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:04.320
Step two files the high and
critical vulnerabilities,

110
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.198
by vendor, software
and recommended update.

111
00:07:07.198 --> 00:07:11.386
One caution the guide records: Learn's
table reference doesn't list the

112
00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:16.009
severity strings, so the guide uses
critical and high as the portal shows them.

113
00:07:16.009 --> 00:07:20.422
If the file comes back empty, check the
values in your tenant before you trust it.

114
00:07:20.422 --> 00:07:24.794
Step three files the exposed and
internet-facing Windows devices,

115
00:07:24.794 --> 00:07:27.497
with their asset value and machine group.

116
00:07:27.497 --> 00:07:30.226
The guide's pass: every
row has an owner in the

117
00:07:30.226 --> 00:07:34.000
decision record and a ticket
in the remediation queue.

118
00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:40.312
Step four exports the open incidents.

119
00:07:40.312 --> 00:07:45.630
The filter asks for incidents whose status
is active, and the export keeps the I D,

120
00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:49.900
the name, the severity, who it's
assigned to, and when it was created.

121
00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:54.062
The second command reads the file
back, so you can see the owner column.

122
00:07:54.062 --> 00:07:57.438
The guide's pass: every
open incident has an owner.

123
00:07:57.438 --> 00:08:00.576
Step five exports one
incident with its alerts.

124
00:08:00.576 --> 00:08:03.275
Use an incident number
from step four's output.

125
00:08:03.275 --> 00:08:07.052
Microsoft Learn's page shows
the expand property for alerts,

126
00:08:07.052 --> 00:08:11.281
so the file carries every alert in
the incident and its evidence, and,

127
00:08:11.281 --> 00:08:15.662
once the incident is resolved, its
classification and determination.

128
00:08:15.662 --> 00:08:19.325
That file is what an assessor
traces from alert to closure.

129
00:08:19.325 --> 00:08:20.863
Step six backs it out.

130
00:08:20.863 --> 00:08:24.051
Nothing changed in the tenant,
so you only disconnect.

131
00:08:24.051 --> 00:08:27.630
Keep the evidence folder with
the rest of the path's files.

132
00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.692
The federal requirement, from
the guide's requirements table.

133
00:08:34.692 --> 00:08:39.982
Gate five supports R A five, vulnerability
monitoring and scanning, S I two,

134
00:08:39.982 --> 00:08:44.491
flaw remediation, and C M eight,
the system component inventory,

135
00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:49.954
all from NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three, revision five.

136
00:08:49.954 --> 00:08:54.405
The evidence is the vulnerability
query output and the remediation queue.

137
00:08:54.405 --> 00:08:59.904
Gate six supports the incident response
family: I R four, incident handling;

138
00:08:59.904 --> 00:09:04.250
I R five, incident monitoring;
and I R six, incident reporting;

139
00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.330
with A U six, audit record
review, analysis and reporting.

140
00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:12.317
The evidence is the incident
exports, with their classification.

141
00:09:12.317 --> 00:09:16.423
Each row lines up with fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicators,

142
00:09:16.423 --> 00:09:21.692
which bind cloud service providers; the
agency lines its own evidence up with them.

143
00:09:21.692 --> 00:09:23.782
The gates support these controls,

144
00:09:23.782 --> 00:09:27.360
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

145
00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.085
Check yourself with two questions.

146
00:09:33.085 --> 00:09:38.197
First: a G C C agency wants attack
paths for its domain controllers.

147
00:09:38.197 --> 00:09:39.623
What does the program do?

148
00:09:39.623 --> 00:09:40.571
The answer is b.

149
00:09:40.571 --> 00:09:44.761
Microsoft Learn says Exposure
Management is Public Cloud only,

150
00:09:44.761 --> 00:09:49.346
so record it as not applicable, and
use the interim path: exposure level,

151
00:09:49.346 --> 00:09:54.089
internet-facing devices, Vulnerability
Management's recommendations,

152
00:09:54.089 --> 00:09:57.647
and the Tier zero inventory
as the critical-asset list.

153
00:09:57.647 --> 00:10:03.360
Second: during a high incident, a playbook
proposes isolating a domain controller.

154
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.372
What happens?

155
00:10:04.372 --> 00:10:05.597
The answer is b.

156
00:10:05.597 --> 00:10:06.973
A human decides.

157
00:10:06.973 --> 00:10:10.023
Servers, and privileged
or emergency accounts,

158
00:10:10.023 --> 00:10:13.698
are never contained by a
playbook, whatever the severity.

159
00:10:13.698 --> 00:10:18.860
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

160
00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:23.435
To sum up.

161
00:10:23.435 --> 00:10:27.563
You recorded Exposure Management
as not applicable in G C C,

162
00:10:27.563 --> 00:10:29.922
and used the interim path instead.

163
00:10:29.922 --> 00:10:32.931
You filed the vulnerability
and exposure queries,

164
00:10:32.931 --> 00:10:36.584
and checked that each exposed
device has an owner and a ticket.

165
00:10:36.584 --> 00:10:38.792
And you exported the open incidents,

166
00:10:38.792 --> 00:10:41.660
and one incident with its
alerts, for the record.

167
00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:44.462
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

168
00:10:44.462 --> 00:10:48.276
the vulnerability query output
and the remediation queue;

169
00:10:48.276 --> 00:10:52.168
the severity table and the
incident response plan's version;

170
00:10:52.168 --> 00:10:56.915
the monthly incident export, with
alerts, classification and timestamps;

171
00:10:56.915 --> 00:11:00.885
and every incident closed outside
its window, with the reason.

172
00:11:00.885 --> 00:11:07.420
Next is Module 4: hunting, detections as
code, and identity and cloud-app signals.
